Depends on 01-systemd-optional.patch (introduces get_option('systemd')).

Supply a runit-based replacement for the disabled leader-systemd.c when
building with -Dsystemd=false. Upstream's leader-systemd.c asks a running
systemd --user instance to activate gnome-session@<name>.target and leans
entirely on that unit's dependency graph (BindsTo/Requires/Wants across
gnome-session-*.target, org.gnome.Shell@user.service, gsd-*.service, etc)
to actually launch gnome-shell, gnome-settings-daemon's components, and
other session services -- gnome-session-service (gsm-manager.c) itself
only still spawns "Application"-phase XDG autostart entries directly, not
the compositor or daemons.

There is no systemd --user equivalent to delegate to here, so
leader-runit.c supervises the session's essential processes itself via a
small runit tree. The service definitions are shipped under
LIBEXECDIR/gnome-session/runit-services, but runsv needs a writable
supervise/ directory next to each run script, so each session builds a
private tree under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gnome-session/runit (one directory
per service, with run symlinked to the shipped script) and runs
runsvdir -P on that. On SIGTERM/SIGINT it asks runsv to bring each
service down (`sv -w5 down`) before stopping the supervisor, and only
quits once runsvdir has actually exited.

Sessions may restrict which services they run with a profile file,
LIBEXECDIR/gnome-session/runit-sessions/<session>, listing service names
one per line -- the stand-in for per-session systemd targets such as gdm's
gnome-session@gnome-login.target drop-in. Sessions without a profile run
every shipped service.

The gnome-session-leader-fifo write on shutdown is kept for protocol
compatibility (matches leader-systemd.c/leader-openrc.c) even though
nothing in this package currently reads the far end -- best-effort, not
required for the leader to function.

--- /dev/null
+++ b/gnome-session/leader-runit.c
@@ -0,0 +1,324 @@
